1. Concept of Column-Oriented NoSQL Databases
Column-oriented NoSQL databases store data by columns rather than by rows. Related columns are grouped into column families.
How the column-family model differs from the traditional relational table model:
Relational model: Fixed schema, data stored in rows (all columns of a record together). Joins are common.
Column-family model: Flexible/schema-less (or schema-on-read). Data is stored by column family. A row can have a different set of columns. No joins; denormalization is preferred.
Advantages for large-scale applications:
Excellent for analytical/aggregate queries (only needed columns are read).
High write throughput and compression.
Horizontal scalability and sparse data handling (missing columns take no space).
Suitable for time-series, event logging, and big data workloads.
Limitations:
Poor performance for transactions requiring strong ACID across many rows.
No multi-row joins or complex relational queries.
Data modeling is more complex (must design for access patterns).
Eventual consistency in many systems can lead to temporary inconsistencies.
