10. Real-time Event Logging System (Millions of events/min)
Why column-oriented NoSQL is suitable:
Extremely high write throughput.
Time-series friendly (row key = event_type + timestamp).
Sparse and flexible schema for different event types.
Horizontal scalability.
Conceptual data model (Cassandra preferred for availability):
Table: events
PRIMARY KEY ((event_type, day), timestamp, event_id)
Columns: user_id, payload, source, ...
Consistency: Tunable (usually LOCAL_QUORUM or ONE for pure logging).
Availability: High (replication factor 3, multi-DC if needed).
Scaling strategy: Partition by event_type + day (or hour), add nodes as volume grows, use time-window compaction.
11. Content Management / Blogging Platforms
Challenges of varying and evolving data structures:
Different content types (articles, comments, tags, metadata) have different attributes.
Schema changes are frequent.
High read/write ratios and need for flexible querying.
How a column-family database helps:
Dynamic columns / wide rows can store arbitrary attributes without schema changes.
Column families can separate content, comments, metadata, and user activity.
Versioning supports content history.
Easy to add new fields (e.g., new metadata) without downtime.
12. Counters in Distributed Applications
Implementation:
HBase: Built-in atomic counters (Increment operation) on cells.
Cassandra: Counter columns (special data type) with atomic increments.
Problems when counters are updated concurrently:
Race conditions if not atomic.
Lost updates under high concurrency.
In eventually consistent systems, counters can temporarily diverge and need careful reconciliation.
Over-counting or under-counting during network partitions.
Both systems provide atomic increment operations to solve the concurrent update problem at the single-cell level.
13. TTL (Time-To-Live) in Cassandra / HBase
Concept: Each cell (or entire row in some cases) can have an expiration time. After the TTL expires, the data is automatically marked for deletion and removed during compaction.
Usefulness:
Temporary user sessions.
Cache-like data.
Event data that is only relevant for a limited time (e.g., last 24 hours of logs).
Automatic cleanup without application-level jobs.
Impact on storage management and application design:
Reduces storage growth.
Simplifies application logic (no need to manually delete expired data).
Must account for eventual deletion (data may still be readable until compaction).
Useful for designing time-bounded data models.
14. Real-time Analytics Platform (High write volume, large datasets, geo-distributed, occasional node failures)
Recommendation: Apache Cassandra
Justification:
Architecture: Masterless → excellent availability and no single point of failure.
Consistency: Tunable (can use LOCAL_QUORUM for good balance).
Availability: Designed for multi-data-center deployments.
Scalability: Linear horizontal scaling for both writes and reads.
Write performance: Optimized for high write throughput.
HBase is better when strong consistency and tight Hadoop/HDFS integration are required, but for geo-distributed high-availability analytics, Cassandra is generally superior.
15. Critical Analysis: “Column-oriented NoSQL databases provide scalability and flexibility, but they require careful data modeling.”
True statement. Both HBase and Cassandra offer massive scalability and schema flexibility, but performance heavily depends on design choices.
Poor choices that negatively affect performance:
Partition keys: Bad partition key → hotspots (all traffic to few nodes) or huge partitions.
Row keys: Sequential keys cause write hotspots in HBase.
Column families: Too many column families increase overhead; mixing different access patterns in one family hurts performance.
Replication: Too high RF wastes storage and increases write latency; too low reduces availability.
Query patterns: Designing tables without considering actual queries leads to full scans or inability to answer questions efficiently.
Wide rows: Extremely wide rows (millions of columns) can cause memory and compaction issues.
Conclusion: Scalability and flexibility are real, but only when the data model is designed around the access patterns and the characteristics of the chosen system. Careful modeling is mandatory, not optional.
